Reason was, if I remember correctly, something of a cross between a
chain-gun and a railgun. Something like that, anyway. The thing was
powered by something like a miniature nuclear reactor, which needed to
be kept well-cooled... I think it overheated while the reactor was
held in the <i>ocean</i> (or, if not, that level of cooling was pretty
much appropriate).

It should ignore armor because, quite frankly, its slugs are thrown
with enough force that they'd probably pierce the armor not only
coming but going; that being the case, any shot that would miss if you
counted the character's armor but not if you ignored it (which is to
say, a shot that hit the armor) is pretty damn certain to do damage.
Doing _more_ damage just doesn't cut it (because the point isn't just
that it does an incredible amount of damage; it's that it does an
incredible amount of damage even if you're hiding behind, say, a steel
bulkhead. (Which I think happens in the book.)

I think it should have infinite ammunition- not that such a thing
makes sense in terms of Snow Crash, but just because it would probably
be easier to implement than an _extra_ ammunition type, and fits more
with the nature of the weapon (which I must agree would make a pretty
good quest artifact. Maybe- and I know this doesn't fit with the book
in the slightest- you've got to go take it off Raven? I dunno). I also
think that it should have a cooloff time, which _does_ fit with the
book; if this wouldn't be easily implementable for a normally fired
weapon, you could make it so that invoking it fired it (as some
artifacts already have timeouts for invocation, right?). It should
also be very, very heavy.

Regardless, I think even Rodney would listen to Reason.

